{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0,
      "why": "iterative is the lowest-power family (single shared datapath, no per-stage registers, no k-way parallel adders). It is the only family that can plausibly minimise power_index while meeting the 2^-12 accuracy constraint. data_width 14-18 and n_iter 14-20 bracket the accuracy boundary (need ~12+ bits, so W=14/N=14 is the minimal corner, W=18/N=20 gives margin); angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..2 let the search trade guard-bit area against accuracy. Throughput is Fmax (1 result/cycle), so Fmax must reach 50 MHz, which the small iterative datapath should achieve."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0,
      "why": "unrolled_k produces 1 result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so throughput = Fmax/(ceil(N/k)+3). To hit 50 MSPS it needs a higher Fmax than iterative, and the k-way parallel adder tree raises both area and power. It is therefore a secondary candidate: useful to confirm whether any (k, W, N) point beats the iterative front on power while still meeting throughput, and to populate the accuracy side of the Pareto front. k capped at 4 to keep the parallel adder tree (and hence power) from exploding; data_width/n_iter/guards mirror iterative so the accuracy trade-off is comparable."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec's hard constraints are throughput >= 50 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-12, with the objective to minimise power_index (accuracy as the trade-off). Among my two assigned families, iterative is the natural power-minimiser: one shared datapath, no pipeline registers, no parallel adder tree, so it should have the lowest power_index and the highest Fmax per area. unrolled_k is strictly heavier (k parallel micro-rotations per cycle plus a shared FSM) and its throughput is throttled by ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result, so it can only win if its higher Fmax compensates; I keep it in the search to verify that and to cover the accuracy end of the front. I focus the ranges on the accuracy boundary: data_width 14-18 and n_iter 14-20 span the minimal corner that just clears 2^-12 up to a comfortable margin, while angle_guard (-2..2) and frac_guard (0..2) let Optuna trade guard-bit area against accuracy. Rounding is left to both options since it shifts accuracy by ~1 LSB at negligible cost. I do not push data_width/n_iter to their registry maxima (28/30) because that would waste budget on points that are far above the accuracy constraint and only increase power, which the objective penalises. The 50 MSPS throughput constraint is the binding one for unrolled_k, so I keep k modest (2-4) to keep Fmax high enough that Fmax/(ceil(N/k)+3) can still reach 50."
}

{
